stem: "Which person",
sections: [
{label: "A", title: "Martin, furniture maker (former lawyer)", text: "I spent nineteen years in a City law firm, and for most of that time I was perfectly content. It was only when I started making furniture at weekends – a simple bookcase at first, then a dining table for my sister – that I began to feel the pull of a different life. When the firm announced a round of voluntary redundancies, I took the money and enrolled on a two-year course in fine woodworking. I assumed, with what I can now see was a lawyer's arrogance, that I would be earning a living from commissions within a year of finishing. In reality it was closer to four. Cabinet-making is a craft in which competence is measured in thousands of hours, and no amount of enthusiasm will let you skip them. My first paid pieces were, frankly, not good enough, and I had to redo several at my own expense. Things are much better now. I have a workshop, a waiting list and a reputation in the area for well-made, unfussy pieces. What I hadn't anticipated was how much I would miss the intellectual sparring of legal work. A dovetail joint either fits or it doesn't; there's no one to argue with. Occasionally I find myself drafting imaginary contracts in my head while I sand, just to keep that part of my brain from rusting."},
{label: "B", title: "Grace, software developer (former nurse)", text: "After fourteen years as an A&E nurse I was worn out, but I couldn't imagine doing anything else, and I certainly couldn't imagine explaining to my parents – who had made enormous sacrifices to put me through training – that I wanted to give it up. So I taught myself to code in secret, in the evenings, for almost a year before I said a word to anyone at home. When I finally did, they were far more supportive than I'd feared, though my father still introduces me as 'my daughter, the nurse'. What surprised me most when I got my first developer job was how much of my old work I was still drawing on. Debugging a system under pressure turns out to be remarkably similar to assessing a patient in a crowded emergency department: you gather the symptoms, rule out the most dangerous possibilities first, and stay calm while everyone around you panics. People who change careers are constantly told to 'follow their passion', and I've never found that helpful. I wasn't passionate about software when I started; I was curious about it, and the passion came later, once I was good enough to enjoy it. If I'd waited to feel passionate before acting, I'd still be on a hospital ward."},
{label: "C", title: "Owen, primary teacher (former banker)", text: "I'd been an investment banker for over a decade when I decided to retrain as a primary school teacher. My salary fell by roughly two-thirds overnight, and I'd spent months beforehand dreading the moment it would happen. In practice, it hurt much less than I'd feared. We sold the second car, stopped taking expensive holidays and discovered that we didn't really miss either. The reaction of the people I worked with was more memorable. My manager at the bank, a man I'd always found rather cold, took me out for lunch on my last day and confessed that he had once wanted to teach himself, before his father talked him out of it. He made me promise to stick at it for at least five years. What nobody had prepared me for was how physically draining a day in a classroom would be. In banking, I'd regularly worked fourteen-hour days, and I thought I knew what tiredness was. But thirty seven-year-olds demand a kind of constant, alert attention that leaves you utterly spent by half past three. For my entire first term I was asleep by nine most evenings, which my wife found hilarious. I'm used to it now, though I still look forward to the holidays with an intensity I never felt as a banker."},
{label: "D", title: "Sally, bookseller (former journalist)", text: "I'd been a newspaper journalist for twenty-two years and had watched the industry shrink around me, one round of cuts after another. I knew I ought to leave, but I had no idea what I'd do instead. Then, on a walking holiday in the Welsh borders, I stopped for shelter in a second-hand bookshop during a rainstorm and fell into conversation with the owner. She was in her eighties, wanted to retire and had been unable to find a buyer. By the time the rain stopped, I had more or less agreed to take the place on. My friends thought I'd lost my mind, and in some ways I suppose I had. Running a small shop is far more complicated than it looks: there are accounts to keep, stock to sort and customers who expect you to remember exactly which book they asked about three months ago. But it suits me in a way journalism never really did. The pace is gentler, and I actually get to meet the people who enjoy what I'm selling. If anything, I wish I'd found the courage to leave much earlier – I wasted at least five years in a job I no longer believed in, simply because I was frightened of the alternative."}
],
